.TH FATHOM_ARCHITECTURE 1  "fathom 0.1.0-alpha.1" 
.SH NAME
fathom\-architecture \- The architecture scorecard: five dimensions, one number, and the drift since your last review (`e3.9`)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfathom architecture\fR [\fB\-\-root\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-revision\fR] [\fB\-\-backfill\fR] [\fB\-\-history\fR] [\fB\-\-refresh\fR] [\fB\-\-read\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-explain\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
The architecture scorecard: five dimensions, one number, and the drift since your last review (`e3.9`)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-root\fR \fI<ROOT>\fR [default: .]
The repository to read
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the report as one JSON line on stdout (`fathom\-architecture/1`)
.TP
\fB\-\-revision\fR \fI<TREE>\fR
Score this revision rather than the newest the graph holds: a full tree oid or an unambiguous prefix
.TP
\fB\-\-backfill\fR \fI<N>\fR [default: 0]
Score this many unscored revisions first, newest first, before reporting (`0`: report what is stored).

Each one costs a graph materialisation, which is why the default is none: a trend that recomputed every revision on every run would be a trend nobody runs.
.TP
\fB\-\-history\fR [\fI<N>\fR] [default: 0]
How many scored revisions the trend reads; bare, or `0`, is every row the index holds
.TP
\fB\-\-refresh\fR
Forget every stored scorecard first, and re\-derive what this run needs.

The recovery path for a row that disagrees with its revision — the honest fix for a derived fact is to re\-derive it, not to patch it.
.TP
\fB\-\-read\-only\fR
Do not write anything: read the stored rows and score only what this run needs.

A dashboard polling on a schedule, or anyone reading a store another process is writing.
.TP
\fB\-\-explain\fR
Print the knobs in force — the weights, the curves and their published constants — and exit
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
